{
  "id": "oob-024",
  "pattern": "off-by-one",
  "difficulty_rating": 1650,
  "explanation": "windowSum's loop condition is `i <= start + size`, which visits `size + 1` indices, not `size`. Every window sums one extra element (and divides by the intended `size` anyway), so each reported average is inflated by roughly one item's worth. For [2,4,6,8] with size 2, the first window sums arr[0..2] = 2+4+6=12 (average 6, not the intended (2+4)/2=3), the second sums arr[1..3]=4+6+8=18 (average 9, not 5), and the third reads past the end (arr[4] is undefined, coalesced to 0) landing on 7 by coincidence. The bug always fires; it only looks 'right' on the last window because the extra read happens to be undefined-as-zero there.",
  "prompt": "windowSum computes the sum over a window of `size` elements starting at `start`. Step through movingAverages and predict what it actually returns.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "function windowSum(arr, start, size) {\n  let total = 0;\n  for (let i = start; i <= start + size; i++) {\n    total += arr[i] ?? 0;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\nfunction movingAverages(arr, size) {\n  const result = [];\n  for (let start = 0; start + size <= arr.length; start++) {\n    result.push(windowSum(arr, start, size) / size);\n  }\n  return result;\n}\nconsole.log(movingAverages([2, 4, 6, 8], 2));",
  "interaction": "scrubber",
